What is an Elastic IP? (EIP)

First, think of this:
Imagine you have a house (your EC2 instance)
But your home address keeps changing every time you go out and come back.
To solve this, you go to the government and say:
“Mujhe ek permanent address do — jo har waqt same rahe!”
That permanent address = Elastic IP 

So, What is Elastic IP?
It's a static, public IPv4 address provided by AWS
You can attach it to your EC2 instance
Even if the instance stops or restarts — IP doesn't change


Why do we need EIP?
Normally, when you start an EC2:
It gets a public IP — but it's temporary
Agar instance stop/start kiya — naya IP milta hai

Bad idea if:
      You're hosting a web app
       DNS records are pointed to that IP
       External tools hit your server via IP
So, to avoid this — use Elastic IP 


Use Case:
I deployed a web server on EC2, and I want a fixed IP so customers can always access it, even after restarts.
Elastic IP = solves this problem.
Aap DNS me bhi set kar sakte ho, aur wo IP kabhi change nahi hogi (until you manually release it).



Caution:
AWS gives only 1 free EIP per region
Agar EIP allocate karke use nahi kar rahe — you will be charged
So only allocate when needed and always attach it to a running instance



Interview Tip:
Elastic IP is a static, public IPv4 address in AWS. It allows consistent access to EC2 even after restart or stop/start events. It's useful when you need a permanent public endpoint for your application.
